<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#oto-manguean-languages">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oto-Manguean languages</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oto-Manguean languages are a large family of Indigenous languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, including Zapotec, Mixtec, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a>, Mazahua and Chinantec. They are tonal languages and among the oldest-attested language families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chorotega">Chorotega</a>, also called Mangue, spoken in Nicaragua and Costa Rica until it died out in the colonial era, was the family's southernmost member.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oto-manguean-languages.jpg" alt="Oto-Manguean languages" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noahedits · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Precontact_Otomanguean.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Oto-Manguean family has existed in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> since at least 2000 BCE and probably several thousand years earlier. Some estimates using glottochronology, a controversial method, suggest that Proto-Oto-Manguean split around 4400 BCE. The Tehuacán Valley has been proposed as the original home, and although this is now uncertain, the Tehuacán culture (5000 BCE to 2300 BCE) was likely made up of Oto-Manguean speakers.</p><p>The internal diversity of the family is comparable with that of Indo-European. Its speakers were among the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> to form highly complex cultures. Monte Albán, with remains dated as early as 1000 BCE, is believed to have been in continuous use by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a>, and the undeciphered Zapotec script is one of the earliest forms of Mesoamerican writing. In the Post-Classic period the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtecs</a> were prolific artisans and codex painters, while Oto-Manguean cultures of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> were marginalised by the intruding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahuas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The family has two main divisions. The western branch includes Oto-Pamean (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a>, Mazahua, Matlatzinca and Pame), Chinantecan and Tlapanec-Mangue. The eastern branch includes Popolocan (Popoloca, Chocho, Mazatecan and Ixcatec), Zapotecan (Zapotec and Chatino) and Mixtecan-Amuzgoan (Mixtec, Trique, Cuicatec and Amuzgo). The largest numbers of speakers live in the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, where Zapotecan and Mixtecan languages are spoken by almost 1.5 million people combined.</p><p>Every Oto-Manguean language is tonal, which makes the family unique among those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. Tone distinguishes the meanings of roots and marks grammatical categories. Some languages have two level tones and others up to ten tone contrasts. The languages are also more analytic than other Mesoamerican languages and almost all use verb-subject-object order. Whistled speech, which carries the tones of words over distances, is common in Chinantec, Mazatec and the Zapotecan languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>A relationship between Zapotecan and Mixtecan was first proposed by Manuel Orozco y Berra in 1864. Later scholars added further groups, and the Oto-Pamean and Oaxacan groups were joined into one family in Edward Sapir's classification of 1929, which placed them in Hokan. Reconstruction work began in the 1950s, with Proto-Oto-Pamean reconstructed by Doris Bartholomew and Proto-Zapotecan by Morris Swadesh. Lyle Campbell's classification of 1997 was the first to give a unified view of the family.</p><p>The family is widely accepted by specialists, though a reconstruction of Proto-Oto-Manguean remains incomplete: only about 350 vocabulary items have been reconstructed. In 2015 the linguist Brown argued that the reconstructed vocabulary lacks regular sound correspondences and proposed that Oto-Manguean might instead be a Sprachbund. Several languages are endangered or extinct. All Manguean languages have died out, and Chiapanec was declared extinct after 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oto-Manguean languages” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
